/**
 * expand-book-05-part12.js
 * Expands Book 5 Chapters 1-8 (Parts 1 & 2) with deep, chapter-specific content.
 * Each chapter gets ~1,700 additional words of unique academic material.
 * NO EM DASHES. All content uses commas, semicolons, or parentheses.
 */
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const bookPath = path.join(__dirname, 'books', 'book-05.json');
const book = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(bookPath, 'utf-8'));

function append(chapter, html) {
  chapter.content = chapter.content + html;
}

// ============ CHAPTER 1 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[0], `
<h2>1.6 The Literature Review as Intellectual Training</h2>
<p>Beyond its functional role in a thesis or journal article, the literature review is one of the most powerful intellectual training exercises in all of academia. The process of reading, evaluating, and synthesising dozens or hundreds of scholarly sources develops cognitive skills that no other academic activity can replicate. It teaches the researcher to hold multiple, often conflicting perspectives in mind simultaneously, to evaluate evidence against rigorous methodological standards, and to construct a coherent narrative from fragmented and sometimes contradictory information (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The literature review also trains the researcher in the art of academic humility. By immersing themselves in the work of others, the researcher comes to appreciate the immense collective effort that has produced the current state of knowledge. They learn that their own contribution, however important, is a small addition to an enormous edifice. This perspective is essential for maintaining intellectual honesty and avoiding the arrogance that sometimes afflicts researchers who believe their work exists in isolation from what came before (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>Furthermore, the process of writing a literature review forces the researcher to develop their academic voice. As they move from describing what others have said to analysing, evaluating, and synthesising, they necessarily begin to assert their own scholarly perspective. The literature review is often the first piece of academic writing in which a doctoral student truly finds their voice, transitioning from a consumer of knowledge to a producer of it. This transformation is not merely technical; it is intellectual and even emotional, marking a fundamental shift in the researcher's identity (Kamler and Thomson, 2014).</p>

<h2>1.7 Disciplinary Variations in the Literature Review</h2>
<p>While the core principles of the literature review are universal across disciplines, the specific expectations, conventions, and norms vary significantly. In the natural sciences, literature reviews tend to be relatively brief, highly focused, and embedded in the introduction section of a research paper. The emphasis is on identifying the specific gap in empirical knowledge that the study will address. Theoretical discussion is typically minimal, and the review is expected to be current, with most cited sources published within the past five to ten years (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>In the social sciences, literature reviews are typically more expansive and more theoretical. They are often standalone chapters in a thesis, and they are expected to engage with both empirical findings and theoretical frameworks. The review must demonstrate not only what is known about a phenomenon but also how it has been conceptualised and theorised. Historical sources may be cited alongside contemporary ones, and the review may trace the development of a concept or debate over several decades (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>In the humanities, the concept of a "literature review" is often less formalised. Scholars in fields such as philosophy, history, or literary criticism may engage with the work of others throughout their text rather than in a dedicated chapter. The engagement is often more interpretive and less systematic than in the social or natural sciences. However, the underlying principle remains the same: the scholar must demonstrate familiarity with the relevant body of scholarship and position their own work within it (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>Understanding these disciplinary differences is essential because a literature review that would be considered excellent in one discipline might be considered inadequate in another. A medical researcher who writes a 30-page theoretical literature review will be told to cut it drastically. A sociology doctoral student who writes a two-page literature review embedded in the introduction will be told to expand it enormously. The researcher must understand the norms of their own discipline and write accordingly (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>

<h2>1.8 Worked Example: Identifying the Nature of a Literature Review</h2>
<p>Consider the following scenario. A master's student in education is studying the impact of digital technology on reading comprehension in primary schools. They have read forty articles on the topic and produced the following paragraph:</p>
<p>"Smith (2019) conducted a study of 200 primary school students and found that students who used tablets scored 12% higher on reading comprehension tests than students who used printed textbooks. However, Jones (2020) studied 150 students in a different district and found no significant difference between tablet users and textbook users. Meanwhile, Lee (2021) found that students who used tablets showed improved engagement but no improvement in comprehension scores."</p>
<p>This paragraph is a summary, not a synthesis. It describes three studies in isolation without analysing the patterns, contradictions, or implications. Now consider the same information presented as synthesis:</p>
<p>"The relationship between digital technology and reading comprehension in primary schools remains contested in the literature. While some studies have reported significant improvements in comprehension scores among tablet users (Smith, 2019), others have found no significant effect (Jones, 2020; Lee, 2021). Notably, Lee's (2021) finding that tablets improve engagement without improving comprehension suggests that the relationship between technology and learning outcomes may be more complex than a simple input-output model implies. The discrepancy in findings may be partly explained by differences in implementation: Smith's study involved a structured, curriculum-aligned tablet programme, while Jones and Lee examined unstructured tablet use. This pattern suggests that the impact of digital technology on comprehension is mediated by pedagogical design, a variable that has been insufficiently examined in the existing literature."</p>
<p>The difference is immediately apparent. The synthesis paragraph identifies a pattern (contested findings), analyses the contradiction (engagement versus comprehension), offers an explanation (implementation differences), and points toward a gap (the mediating role of pedagogical design). This is the kind of analytical writing that examiners want to see in every paragraph of a literature review (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>1.9 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>This chapter has established five foundational principles for the literature review. First, a literature review is a critical, analytical synthesis of existing scholarship, not a summary. Second, it serves three core functions: demonstrating knowledge, establishing foundations, and identifying gaps. Third, it is structured as an argument, with a thesis, evidence, and logical structure. Fourth, it operates differently in different contexts (thesis chapters, journal articles, proposals, standalone publications). Fifth, it is an ongoing process that evolves throughout the research journey. With these principles firmly in mind, you are now ready to explore the different types of literature reviews that exist and learn how to select the one most appropriate for your research (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 2 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[1], `
<h2>2.7 Rapid Evidence Assessments and Other Emerging Types</h2>
<p>Beyond the four major types discussed above, several newer forms of literature review have emerged in response to specific needs. The Rapid Evidence Assessment (REA) is a truncated form of systematic review designed to provide timely evidence for policy decisions. It uses the same systematic principles (explicit search strategy, defined inclusion criteria, quality assessment) but applies them with a reduced scope, often limiting the search to fewer databases or a shorter time period. REAs sacrifice some comprehensiveness for speed, making them useful when decision-makers cannot wait for a full systematic review (Thomas et al., 2013).</p>
<p>The Critical Interpretive Synthesis (CIS) is another emerging form that combines the rigour of systematic searching with the interpretive depth of traditional narrative review. Developed by Dixon-Woods et al. (2006), CIS was designed specifically for topics where the literature is diverse in methodology and theoretical orientation, making traditional meta-analysis impossible. CIS allows the reviewer to develop new conceptual frameworks from the literature, rather than simply summarising existing ones.</p>
<p>The Realist Review (also known as a Realist Synthesis) is a theory-driven approach developed by Pawson et al. (2005). Rather than asking "Does this intervention work?", a realist review asks "What works, for whom, in what circumstances, and why?" This approach is particularly useful for evaluating complex social programmes where context and mechanisms of change are as important as outcomes. Realist reviews are increasingly popular in health services research, education, and social policy (Rycroft-Malone et al., 2012).</p>
<p>The Umbrella Review (or Overview of Reviews) synthesises evidence from existing systematic reviews rather than from primary studies. It is used when multiple systematic reviews already exist on a topic and there is a need to integrate their findings at a higher level. Umbrella reviews are particularly useful for broad clinical or policy questions where the evidence base is large and well-reviewed (Aromataris et al., 2015).</p>

<h2>2.8 Comparing Review Types: A Decision Matrix</h2>
<p>Selecting the right type of review requires careful consideration of several factors. The following comparison illustrates how these factors align with different review types. Consider first the nature of your research question. If it is broad and exploratory (for example, "What is known about student engagement in online learning?"), a scoping review or narrative review is appropriate. If it is specific and focused (for example, "Does flipped classroom pedagogy improve examination scores in undergraduate biology courses?"), a systematic review is more suitable (Grant and Booth, 2009).</p>
<p>Consider next the state of the existing literature. If the literature is diverse, multidisciplinary, and theoretically complex, a narrative review or critical interpretive synthesis will serve you best. If the literature consists primarily of comparable empirical studies with quantifiable outcomes, a systematic review with meta-analysis is the gold standard. If you are unsure about the extent and nature of the literature, a scoping review can serve as a preliminary step before committing to a full systematic review (Peters et al., 2020).</p>
<p>Consider also the purpose of your review within your broader research project. If the review is a chapter in a thesis, a narrative review is almost always expected (though some doctoral programmes now accept systematic reviews as standalone chapters). If the review is intended for journal publication as a piece of original research, a systematic review or meta-analysis carries more weight and is more likely to be accepted by high-impact journals (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
<p>Finally, consider the practical resources available to you. Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are resource-intensive, often requiring a team of reviewers, specialised software (such as Covidence or RevMan), and months of dedicated work. A narrative review can be conducted by a single researcher and is more flexible in its timeline. Scoping reviews fall somewhere in between. The choice of review type must be pragmatic as well as methodological (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>2.9 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>This chapter has established that literature reviews are not monolithic; they come in multiple forms, each designed for specific purposes and governed by specific methodological conventions. The narrative review offers flexibility and interpretive depth but is susceptible to selection bias. The systematic review offers rigour and reproducibility but requires significant resources and a specific, focused question. Meta-analysis adds statistical power to systematic reviews by pooling quantitative results. Scoping reviews map broad fields and identify research priorities. Emerging forms such as rapid evidence assessments, critical interpretive syntheses, and realist reviews address specific needs that traditional forms do not fully meet. Your task as a researcher is to select the form that best serves your research question, justify that selection in your methodology, and execute the review to the standards required by your chosen type (Grant and Booth, 2009).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 3 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[2], `
<h2>3.6 The Literature Review and Theory Building</h2>
<p>One of the most sophisticated functions of the literature review is its role in theory building. In fields where established theories are contested or insufficient, the literature review can serve as the foundation for constructing new theoretical propositions. By systematically analysing the patterns, contradictions, and gaps in the existing literature, the reviewer can identify relationships between concepts that have not been previously articulated and propose new theoretical frameworks that integrate disparate findings into a coherent whole (Torraco, 2005).</p>
<p>This theory-building function is distinct from the more common theory-testing function. In theory-testing research (the dominant mode in quantitative social science), the literature review identifies an existing theory, derives testable hypotheses from it, and designs a study to test those hypotheses. In theory-building research, the literature review is itself the primary analytical tool. The reviewer examines the literature not to find a theory to test but to construct a theory that explains what the literature collectively reveals. This approach is common in management science, organisational studies, and information systems, where integrative reviews are published as original theoretical contributions (Webster and Watson, 2002).</p>
<p>The integrative literature review, as described by Torraco (2005), is specifically designed for this purpose. It reviews, critiques, and synthesises representative literature on a topic in an integrated way such that new frameworks and perspectives are generated. Unlike a systematic review, which aims to be comprehensive, an integrative review selects sources strategically to build the most compelling theoretical argument. The product of an integrative review is not a summary of findings but a new conceptual model or theoretical proposition that advances understanding of the phenomenon.</p>

<h2>3.7 Practical Application: Adapting Your Review to Your Research Design</h2>
<p>To illustrate how the literature review must adapt to the research paradigm, consider three doctoral students all studying the phenomenon of academic burnout among postgraduate researchers. The first student is conducting a quantitative survey study. Their literature review will identify the key variables (workload, supervisor support, institutional culture, coping strategies), review the empirical evidence for relationships between these variables, present the theoretical framework (perhaps Conservation of Resources theory), and derive specific hypotheses (for example, "Supervisor support will moderate the relationship between workload and burnout"). The review will be deductive, moving from general findings to specific predictions (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>
<p>The second student is conducting a phenomenological study. Their literature review will explore how burnout has been conceptualised and experienced, review the limited qualitative literature on postgraduate researcher wellbeing, and identify the experiential dimensions of burnout that have not been adequately explored. The review will be more tentative and exploratory, avoiding the imposition of predetermined categories. It will conclude not with hypotheses but with a research question focused on understanding the lived experience of burnout from the participants' own perspectives (Creswell and Poth, 2018).</p>
<p>The third student is conducting a mixed methods study, using a survey followed by in-depth interviews. Their literature review will need to serve both functions: providing the quantitative framework for the survey design and the conceptual sensitivity for the qualitative interviews. The review may be structured in two sections, one addressing the empirical evidence on burnout predictors and the other exploring the experiential and contextual dimensions that quantitative measures cannot capture. The justification for mixed methods will emerge from the review itself, demonstrating that neither approach alone can fully capture the complexity of the phenomenon (Creswell and Plano Clark, 2018).</p>

<h2>3.8 Common Mistakes in Aligning the Review with the Research Paradigm</h2>
<p>Several common mistakes arise when researchers fail to align their literature review with their research paradigm. The most frequent is the quantitative student who writes a purely descriptive, narrative review without connecting the literature to specific variables and hypotheses. The review reads as an interesting essay on the topic but does not build the logical foundation for a testable study. Examiners will note that the review fails to operationalise concepts or justify predictions (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>
<p>Equally problematic is the qualitative student who writes a literature review that is too prescriptive, imposing a rigid framework on a phenomenon that the study is supposed to explore with fresh eyes. If a phenomenological study begins with a detailed model of exactly what the researcher expects to find, the study has been compromised before data collection begins. The literature review should sensitise, not prescribe (Charmaz, 2014).</p>
<p>A third mistake is the mixed methods student who treats the literature review as two separate, disconnected sections rather than an integrated whole. The power of mixed methods research lies in its ability to combine different forms of evidence. The literature review must model this integration, showing how quantitative and qualitative perspectives on the phenomenon complement each other and why both are necessary to achieve a comprehensive understanding (Tashakkori and Teddlie, 2010).</p>

<h2>3.9 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>The literature review is not a one-size-fits-all document. Its purpose, structure, and relationship to the broader study vary significantly depending on the research paradigm, the type of research question, and the specific research design. Quantitative studies require deductive reviews that lead to hypotheses. Qualitative studies require exploratory reviews that sensitise without prescribing. Mixed methods studies require integrated reviews that justify the combination of approaches. Understanding these distinctions is essential for producing a literature review that genuinely serves your study rather than existing as an isolated academic exercise (Ravitch and Riggan, 2017).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 4 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[3], `
<h2>4.7 Outdated and Narrow Source Selection</h2>
<p>A literature review that relies heavily on outdated sources sends a clear signal to examiners: the researcher has not kept up with recent developments in their field. While foundational and seminal works should certainly be included regardless of their publication date, the majority of sources in a literature review should be recent, typically published within the past ten years. If the most recent source in a review is five years old, the examiner will question whether the researcher is aware of current debates, methodological advances, and emerging findings. In fast-moving fields such as technology, health sciences, and artificial intelligence, even sources from three years ago may be considered dated (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>A related problem is narrow source selection, where the reviewer draws exclusively from one type of source (for example, only journal articles) or one geographical context (for example, only studies conducted in the United States). A comprehensive literature review should engage with a range of source types, including journal articles, books, book chapters, conference papers, reports, and (where appropriate) grey literature. It should also seek international perspectives, especially when the phenomenon under study is not culture-specific (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>4.8 Failure to Engage with Contradictory Evidence</h2>
<p>One of the most telling signs of a weak literature review is the absence of contradictory evidence. If every source cited supports the same conclusion, the examiner will suspect that the reviewer has selectively included only studies that support their predetermined position and excluded those that challenge it. This confirmation bias is one of the most serious methodological sins in academic research (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>A strong literature review actively seeks out and engages with contradictory evidence. When different studies reach different conclusions, the reviewer must analyse why. Are the contradictions due to differences in methodology (for example, qualitative versus quantitative approaches)? Are they due to differences in context (for example, studies conducted in different cultural settings)? Are they due to differences in how key concepts were operationalised? By analysing the sources of contradiction, the reviewer demonstrates sophisticated critical thinking and often identifies the most productive gaps for new research (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>
<p>The ability to engage with contradictory evidence is also a signal of intellectual maturity. A novice researcher is uncomfortable with contradiction and tries to resolve it by ignoring one side. A mature researcher understands that contradiction is the lifeblood of academic progress and that the most interesting research questions often emerge from precisely those areas where the literature disagrees. Your literature review should not shy away from disagreement; it should embrace it as an opportunity for analytical depth (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>4.9 Worked Example: Transforming a Weak Review into a Strong One</h2>
<p>Consider a literature review paragraph from a master's thesis on employee engagement in remote work environments. The original version reads: "Johnson (2019) found that remote workers report higher job satisfaction. Williams (2020) found that remote workers are more productive. Chen (2021) found that remote workers have better work-life balance." This is a classic laundry list: three sources, each summarised in a single sentence, with no analysis, no synthesis, and no critical evaluation.</p>
<p>The improved version reads: "The literature on remote work consistently reports positive outcomes for individual employees, including higher job satisfaction (Johnson, 2019), increased productivity (Williams, 2020), and improved work-life balance (Chen, 2021). However, these findings must be interpreted with caution. All three studies relied on self-reported measures, which are susceptible to social desirability bias; employees may overstate the benefits of remote work because they fear losing the privilege if they report negative experiences. Furthermore, these studies examined individual-level outcomes while largely ignoring team-level and organisational-level effects. Recent research by Patel (2022) suggests that while individual remote workers report higher satisfaction, their teams report lower cohesion and more communication breakdowns. This gap between individual and collective outcomes represents a significant lacuna in the current literature that warrants further investigation."</p>
<p>The transformation is substantial. The improved paragraph synthesises findings around a theme, evaluates methodological limitations, introduces contradictory evidence, and identifies a specific gap. It demonstrates the kind of critical, analytical thinking that examiners reward (Ridley, 2012).</p>

<h2>4.10 A Comprehensive Self-Assessment Framework</h2>
<p>Before submitting your literature review, apply the following diagnostic framework. At the structural level: Is the review organised thematically rather than author-by-author? Does each section build logically on the previous one? Is there a clear introduction that sets the scope and a clear conclusion that states the gap? At the analytical level: Does each paragraph contain synthesis rather than mere summary? Are sources compared and contrasted rather than simply listed? Are methodological strengths and weaknesses of key studies explicitly evaluated? At the evidential level: Is the source selection comprehensive, current, and balanced? Are contradictory findings acknowledged and analysed? Are seminal works cited alongside recent contributions? At the argumentative level: Does the review build a coherent, progressive argument? Does the argument lead inexorably to the identification of a specific research gap? Does the gap statement connect directly to your research questions or hypotheses? A review that passes this diagnostic framework at all four levels is ready for submission. A review that fails at any level requires further revision before it can be considered complete (Hart, 2018).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 5 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[4], `
<h2>5.6 The PICO and PEO Frameworks for Defining Scope</h2>
<p>Structured frameworks can help researchers define the scope of their literature review with precision. The PICO framework, widely used in health sciences, specifies four elements: Population (who is being studied?), Intervention (what treatment or exposure is being examined?), Comparison (what is the alternative?), and Outcome (what result is being measured?). A PICO-structured question might be: "In primary school children (P), does tablet-based reading instruction (I), compared to traditional print-based instruction (C), improve reading comprehension scores (O)?" This structured question immediately defines the scope of the literature search and makes it clear what types of studies should be included and excluded (Richardson et al., 1995).</p>
<p>For qualitative and social science research, the PEO framework (Population, Exposure, Outcome) or the SPIDER framework (Sample, Phenomenon of Interest, Design, Evaluation, Research type) may be more appropriate. These frameworks accommodate the less structured nature of qualitative research questions while still providing a systematic basis for defining scope. For example, a PEO-structured question might be: "Among first-generation university students (P), how does the experience of academic mentoring (E) influence persistence to graduation (O)?" (Cooke, Smith, and Booth, 2012).</p>
<p>Regardless of which framework you use, the key principle is the same: the scope of your review must be defined explicitly, systematically, and in advance of the search. Ad hoc decisions about what to include and exclude, made during the search process, introduce bias and undermine the credibility of the review. A well-defined scope is your most powerful defence against the twin dangers of drowning in too much literature and producing a review that is too superficial to be useful (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>5.7 Practical Exercise: Defining the Scope for Your Own Review</h2>
<p>To apply the principles discussed in this chapter, work through the following exercise using your own research topic. First, write your research question as clearly and specifically as possible. Second, identify the key concepts in your question and list the synonyms, related terms, and alternative spellings for each concept. Third, define your conceptual boundaries: what topics are included and what topics are explicitly excluded? Write a brief justification for each exclusion. Fourth, define your temporal boundaries: will you limit your search to a specific time period? If so, why? Fifth, define your geographical or contextual boundaries: will you limit your search to studies conducted in specific countries, populations, or settings? Again, justify each boundary (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>Sixth, specify your source type boundaries: will you include only peer-reviewed journal articles, or will you also include books, conference papers, theses, and grey literature? The decision should be guided by the norms of your discipline and the nature of your question. Seventh, specify your language boundaries: will you include only English-language sources, or will you search in other languages? If you exclude non-English sources, acknowledge this as a limitation. Eighth, compile all of these decisions into a single scope statement of approximately 200 to 400 words that can be included in your methodology or at the beginning of your literature review chapter. This scope statement serves as a contract with your reader: it tells them exactly what the review covers and why (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>5.8 Managing Scope Creep During the Review Process</h2>
<p>Even with well-defined boundaries, scope creep is a constant danger during the literature review process. As you read more widely, you will inevitably encounter interesting tangential topics, fascinating methodological debates, and compelling findings that are adjacent to, but not squarely within, your defined scope. The temptation to include these materials is strong, especially when you have invested time in reading and understanding them (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The most effective strategy for managing scope creep is to maintain a separate document for "interesting but out of scope" sources. When you encounter a source that does not fit your inclusion criteria but seems potentially relevant, add it to this document rather than to your main literature database. At the end of your search, review this document to determine whether any sources genuinely warrant a revision of your scope. If they do, revise your scope statement explicitly and document the change. If they do not, set them aside with a clear conscience, knowing that you have maintained the integrity of your search boundaries (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>Another effective strategy is to conduct your review in two phases. In the first phase, conduct a broad, exploratory search to understand the landscape of the literature. In the second phase, refine your scope based on what you have learned and conduct a focused, systematic search within your defined boundaries. This two-phase approach allows you to make informed decisions about scope while still maintaining the rigour that examiners expect (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>5.9 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>Defining the scope of your literature review is a methodological decision that must be made deliberately and transparently. Use structured frameworks (PICO, PEO, SPIDER) to define your scope with precision. Set conceptual, temporal, geographical, source type, and language boundaries. Document all scope decisions in a clear scope statement. Manage scope creep by maintaining a separate document for out-of-scope sources and by conducting your review in two phases. A well-scoped review demonstrates methodological rigour and protects you from the paralysis of trying to read everything (Hart, 2018).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 6 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[5], `
<h2>6.6 Advanced Keyword Development Techniques</h2>
<p>Developing an effective set of search terms requires more than simply identifying the obvious keywords in your research question. Advanced keyword development involves a systematic process of expanding and refining your search vocabulary to ensure that you capture the full range of relevant literature. Begin by identifying the core concepts in your research question and listing all possible synonyms, related terms, and variant spellings for each concept. For example, if your research question involves "employee motivation," your keyword list should include terms such as "worker motivation," "staff motivation," "employee engagement," "job satisfaction," "intrinsic motivation," "extrinsic motivation," "work motivation," and "organisational commitment" (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Next, consider disciplinary variations in terminology. Different academic fields often use different terms for the same concept. What psychologists call "self-efficacy," education researchers might call "academic confidence." What sociologists call "social capital," organisational scientists might call "network resources." If your research spans multiple disciplines, you must include the terminology of each discipline in your search strategy. Failure to do so will result in missing entire bodies of relevant literature simply because they use different vocabulary (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>Controlled vocabulary and subject headings offer another powerful tool for keyword development. Major databases such as PubMed (which uses Medical Subject Headings, or MeSH) and PsycINFO (which uses the Thesaurus of Psychological Index Terms) assign standardised subject headings to each article. Searching by subject heading can capture articles that use different terminology but address the same concept. For example, searching the MeSH heading "Burnout, Professional" in PubMed will retrieve all articles indexed under that heading, regardless of whether the article itself uses the term "burnout," "occupational exhaustion," or "compassion fatigue" (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>Finally, iterative refinement is essential. Your initial search will almost certainly need to be adjusted based on the results. If the search returns too many irrelevant results, you need to add more specific terms or additional Boolean restrictions. If it returns too few results, you need to broaden your terms or remove restrictions. This iterative process should be documented at each stage, recording the exact search string used, the database searched, the date of the search, and the number of results returned (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>6.7 Worked Example: Building a Complete Search Strategy</h2>
<p>To illustrate the process, consider a doctoral student researching the impact of peer mentoring on first-year university retention. The research question is: "Does structured peer mentoring improve retention rates among first-year undergraduate students in UK universities?" The student begins by identifying three core concepts: (1) peer mentoring, (2) student retention, and (3) first-year undergraduates.</p>
<p>For Concept 1 (peer mentoring), the keyword list includes: "peer mentoring," "peer tutoring," "peer support," "peer coaching," "student mentoring," "buddy system," "peer-assisted learning," and "peer-led intervention." For Concept 2 (student retention), the list includes: "student retention," "student persistence," "student attrition," "dropout," "withdrawal," "first-year retention," "student success," and "completion rates." For Concept 3 (first-year undergraduates), the list includes: "first-year students," "freshmen," "first-year undergraduates," "new students," "incoming students," and "Year 1 students" (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The search string combines these concepts using Boolean operators: ("peer mentoring" OR "peer tutoring" OR "peer support" OR "peer coaching" OR "student mentoring" OR "buddy system" OR "peer-assisted learning") AND ("student retention" OR "student persistence" OR "student attrition" OR "dropout" OR "withdrawal" OR "completion rates") AND ("first-year" OR "freshm*" OR "Year 1" OR "incoming student*" OR "new student*"). This comprehensive search string is then applied to multiple databases, including Scopus, Web of Science, ERIC, and the British Education Index, with results documented at each step (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>6.8 Adapting Your Strategy Across Databases</h2>
<p>A critical but often overlooked aspect of search strategy development is the need to adapt your search string for different databases. Each database has its own syntax for Boolean operators, truncation symbols, phrase searching, and field codes. A search string that works perfectly in Scopus may produce errors or unexpected results in PubMed or ERIC. The researcher must understand the specific conventions of each database and modify the search string accordingly (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>For example, Scopus uses the asterisk (*) for truncation and allows proximity operators (such as W/n for "within n words"), while PubMed uses the asterisk for truncation but does not support proximity searching in the same way. ERIC uses the EBSCO interface, which supports both truncation and proximity operators but uses different syntax (N/n for "near within n words"). Google Scholar does not support Boolean operators in the traditional sense and relies instead on its own algorithm to determine relevance. Understanding these differences and documenting how you adapted your search for each database is essential for ensuring reproducibility and demonstrating methodological rigour (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>6.9 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>An effective search strategy is the backbone of a credible literature review. It requires systematic keyword development (including synonyms, disciplinary variants, and controlled vocabulary), strategic use of Boolean operators, careful documentation at every stage, and adaptation for different databases. A poorly constructed search strategy will either miss relevant literature or inundate the researcher with irrelevant results, both of which undermine the quality and credibility of the final review. Invest the time to build a comprehensive, well-documented search strategy before you begin searching, and refine it iteratively as you learn more about the literature landscape (Hart, 2018).</p>
`);

// ============ CHAPTER 7 EXPANSION ============
append(book.chapters[6], `
<h2>7.5 Database Selection for Different Disciplines</h2>
<p>The choice of databases is one of the most important decisions in the literature search process, and it must be justified in your methodology. Different databases index different journals and different types of publications, and no single database provides comprehensive coverage of any field. A literature review that searches only one database, no matter how large, will inevitably miss relevant studies. Best practice requires searching at least two, and preferably three or more, databases relevant to your field (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>For health sciences and biomedical research, the core databases are PubMed (which indexes MEDLINE), Embase, and the Cochrane Library. PubMed is freely accessible and provides excellent coverage of biomedical and life sciences journals. Embase has stronger coverage of European journals, pharmacological literature, and conference proceedings. The Cochrane Library specialises in systematic reviews and clinical trials. For a systematic review in health sciences, searching all three is considered the minimum standard (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>For social sciences, the core databases are Scopus, Web of Science, and PsycINFO (for psychology and behavioural sciences). Scopus offers the broadest coverage of peer-reviewed journals across all disciplines. Web of Science provides access to citation data and impact metrics. PsycINFO is the definitive database for psychological research. For education research, ERIC (Education Resources Information Center) is essential. For business and management, ABI/INFORM and Business Source Complete provide comprehensive coverage (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>For humanities and arts, the databases are more specialised. The MLA International Bibliography covers literature, language, and linguistics. Historical Abstracts covers historical research. PhilPapers covers philosophy. JSTOR provides access to archived journal content across multiple disciplines. In the humanities, books and book chapters are often as important as journal articles, so library catalogues and publishers' databases must also be searched (Ridley, 2012).</p>

<h2>7.6 Grey Literature and Supplementary Sources</h2>
<p>Grey literature refers to research that is not published in peer-reviewed journals or books. It includes theses and dissertations, conference proceedings, government reports, working papers, policy documents, and organisational publications. Grey literature is important because it can provide access to research that is not available through standard database searches. Studies with negative or inconclusive findings are less likely to be published in journals (a phenomenon known as publication bias), but they may be available as theses, conference papers, or working papers (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Key sources of grey literature include ProQuest Dissertations and Theses (for doctoral and master's theses), Google Scholar (which indexes a wide range of non-journal sources), institutional repositories (where universities archive their researchers' outputs), and the websites of relevant organisations, government departments, and international bodies. For systematic reviews, searching grey literature is considered essential to minimise publication bias, and PRISMA guidelines recommend that grey literature searches be documented alongside database searches (Moher et al., 2009).</p>
<p>Snowballing (also known as citation tracking or pearl growing) is another supplementary search technique that should be used alongside database searching. Forward snowballing involves identifying newer articles that cite a key paper (using tools such as Google Scholar's "Cited by" feature or Web of Science's citation report). Backward snowballing involves examining the reference list of a key paper to identify earlier, relevant studies. Snowballing is particularly effective for identifying sources that use different terminology from your search terms and would therefore be missed by keyword-based database searches (Wohlin, 2014).</p>

<h2>7.7 The PRISMA Flow Diagram: Documenting Your Search</h2>
<p>For systematic and scoping reviews, the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) flow diagram is the standard tool for documenting the search and selection process. The flow diagram records the number of records identified through database searching, the number of additional records identified through other sources (such as snowballing and grey literature), the number of records after duplicates were removed, the number of records screened by title and abstract, the number of full-text articles assessed for eligibility, and the number of studies included in the final synthesis. At each stage, the number of records excluded and the reasons for exclusion are documented (Moher et al., 2009).</p>
<p>Even for narrative reviews that are not required to follow PRISMA guidelines, documenting the search process in a systematic way is strongly recommended. A brief description of the databases searched, the search terms used, the date of the search, and the approximate number of results provides transparency and allows the reader (and the examiner) to assess the thoroughness and rigour of the review. This documentation is typically included in the methodology section of a thesis or in a methods paragraph within the literature review itself (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>7.8 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>Conducting a systematic and reproducible database search requires careful database selection, supplementary search techniques (grey literature searching and snowballing), and thorough documentation of every step. Search at least two or three databases relevant to your discipline. Include grey literature to minimise publication bias. Use forward and backward snowballing to capture sources that keyword searches miss. Document the entire process using a PRISMA flow diagram or equivalent. A well-conducted and well-documented search is the foundation of a credible literature review (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
`);

