append(book.chapters[7], `
<h2>8.6 Developing a Formal Eligibility Protocol</h2>
<p>For systematic and scoping reviews, the inclusion and exclusion criteria should be formalised in a protocol document before the search begins. This protocol specifies, in precise and operational terms, the criteria that will be used to determine whether each source is included or excluded from the review. The protocol should be sufficiently detailed that two independent reviewers, applying the criteria to the same set of sources, would reach the same decisions in the vast majority of cases. Where disagreements occur, the protocol should specify a resolution mechanism, such as discussion between reviewers or referral to a third reviewer (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>A formal eligibility protocol typically includes the following elements: the population or context of interest (who or what is being studied); the intervention, exposure, or phenomenon of interest (what is being examined); the comparison or control condition (if applicable); the outcome or outcomes of interest; the study designs that are eligible for inclusion; the time period covered by the search; the languages included; and any other criteria specific to the research question. Each criterion should be accompanied by a clear definition and, where possible, an example of a study that would be included and a study that would be excluded (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>8.7 Pilot Testing Your Criteria</h2>
<p>Before applying your inclusion and exclusion criteria to the full set of search results, it is strongly recommended that you pilot test them on a sample of sources. Select a random sample of 20 to 30 titles and abstracts from your search results and apply your criteria to each one. Note any cases where the criteria are ambiguous, where you are unsure whether a source should be included or excluded, or where the criteria seem to exclude sources that you believe are relevant. Use these observations to refine your criteria before applying them to the full dataset (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>If you are working with a research team, pilot testing is also an opportunity to assess inter-rater reliability, the extent to which different reviewers apply the criteria consistently. Have two or more reviewers independently screen the pilot sample and then compare their decisions. Calculate the percentage agreement or, for more rigorous assessment, Cohen's kappa coefficient. If agreement is low, the criteria need to be clarified or additional training is required. A kappa value of 0.80 or above is generally considered to indicate excellent agreement (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>

<h2>8.8 Worked Example: Developing Criteria for a Literature Review on Online Learning</h2>
<p>Consider a doctoral student conducting a systematic review of the effectiveness of synchronous online learning in higher education. The research question is: "What is the effect of synchronous online learning on academic achievement in undergraduate courses?" The student develops the following eligibility criteria:</p>
<p><strong>Inclusion criteria:</strong> (1) Studies that examine synchronous (real-time) online learning, including video conferencing, live streaming, or virtual classrooms; (2) Studies conducted in higher education settings (undergraduate level); (3) Studies that measure academic achievement using objective assessments (grades, test scores, pass rates); (4) Empirical studies with a comparison or control group (pre-post or comparison with face-to-face instruction); (5) Studies published in English between 2010 and 2025; (6) Peer-reviewed journal articles, conference proceedings, and doctoral theses (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p><strong>Exclusion criteria:</strong> (1) Studies that examine only asynchronous online learning (pre-recorded lectures, discussion forums); (2) Studies conducted in K-12 education, vocational training, or corporate training settings; (3) Studies that measure only student satisfaction, engagement, or perceptions without measuring academic achievement; (4) Purely qualitative studies without measurable achievement outcomes; (5) Studies published before 2010 (as the technology landscape has changed fundamentally since then); (6) Book reviews, editorials, opinion pieces, and non-empirical commentaries (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>These criteria are specific, operational, and justified. They allow the reviewer to screen search results efficiently and consistently, and they provide a transparent basis for the inclusion decisions that can be scrutinised by examiners and peer reviewers. Notice how each criterion directly relates to the research question: the population (undergraduates), the intervention (synchronous online learning), the outcome (academic achievement), and the study design (empirical with comparison group) are all specified (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>

<h2>8.9 Recording Decisions and Building an Audit Trail</h2>
<p>Every inclusion and exclusion decision should be recorded, along with the reason for the decision. For systematic reviews, this information is typically managed using specialised software such as Covidence, Rayyan, or EPPI-Reviewer, which allows reviewers to screen titles and abstracts, record decisions, and track the progress of the selection process. For narrative reviews, a simple spreadsheet with columns for source reference, decision (include/exclude), and reason for exclusion is sufficient (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>This audit trail serves several purposes. It provides transparency, allowing readers to understand exactly how the final set of included sources was determined. It provides accountability, ensuring that the reviewer can justify every inclusion and exclusion decision if challenged. It provides a basis for the PRISMA flow diagram, which requires precise numbers at each stage of the screening process. And it provides a safeguard against bias, because the documented criteria prevent the reviewer from selectively including or excluding sources based on whether their findings support the reviewer's hypothesis (Moher et al., 2009).</p>

<h2>8.10 Key Takeaways</h2>
<p>Inclusion and exclusion criteria are the gatekeeping mechanism of your literature review. They determine which sources enter your review and which are left out, and they must be defined explicitly, applied consistently, and documented transparently. Develop a formal eligibility protocol before you begin searching. Pilot test your criteria on a small sample and refine them as needed. Use structured frameworks (PICO, PEO) to ensure that your criteria are comprehensive and aligned with your research question. Document every decision in an audit trail. Handle borderline cases through team discussion or by applying the principle of maximum inclusion followed by quality assessment. Well-defined criteria protect the integrity of your review and demonstrate methodological rigour to examiners and peer reviewers (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
`);

